course: dictionary
lesson: 15144
title: Healthy scepticism
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Scepticism is a noun. It means doubt about whether something is true, or not believing a claim until there is good evidence. «Ben felt scepticism about the advert that promised his toy would fly.» Ben did not believe it yet, and he wanted proof.
explain: Healthy scepticism means checking facts and asking «how do we know?» before you believe something. It is a good habit, not rudeness. «Mia showed healthy scepticism when she checked the news story on two different websites.»
explain: In stories, a sceptical character doubts magic or ghosts until they see proof. Look for clue words such as doubt, proof, evidence and «how do we know?» to find the meaning of scepticism in a text.
fact: Scepticism means doubt about whether something is true or will work.
fact: Scepticism is the habit of not believing things until there is good evidence.
fact: Common phrases are «healthy scepticism», «treated with scepticism» and «scepticism about».
fact: «Treated with scepticism» means someone did not quite believe it and was careful.
fact: In philosophy, scepticism is the idea that we cannot be sure of some or any knowledge.
exercise: «Sam felt scepticism about the advert that promised a toy would fly.» What does scepticism mean here? | Sleepiness | Anger at the shop | Great excitement about the toy | *Doubt about whether it is true
exercise: «Jo treated the email with scepticism, because it asked for her password.» What does this suggest? | *Jo did not quite believe it and was careful | Jo wrote back a poem | Jo believed every word at once | Jo lost the email
exercise: What is a sign of healthy scepticism? | Believing every advert you see | Repeating a rumour to your friends | *Asking «how do we know?» before believing a claim | Never reading anything online
test: Which word is closest in meaning to scepticism? | Speed | *Doubt | Joy | Sleep
test: «Ella had scepticism about the ghost story, so she looked for proof.» What was Ella looking for? | *Proof, or evidence that it is true | A way to go to sleep | Somewhere to hide | To be scared
exam: Which best describes scepticism? | Forgetting facts quickly | *Not believing a claim until there is good evidence | Being loud in class | Believing everything you hear
